            {
                "entry_id": "5ff1adbc-c77b-32fe-93f4-8d8ef2d77d26",
                "word": "Marmite",
                "pos": "name",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "3dba2126-7cf2-386f-8491-996398bbbf54",
                        "entry_id": "5ff1adbc-c77b-32fe-93f4-8d8ef2d77d26",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A brand of sticky, dark brown, strongly flavored spread based on a yeast extract (a by-product of beer brewing), eaten on toast, in sandwiches, in savory dishes, etc."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "Commonwealth"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "2f2e67b4-e6c3-4ab0-a882-fd2d89b30723",
                                "sense_id": "3dba2126-7cf2-386f-8491-996398bbbf54",
                                "text": "Peyró has the grace to acknowledge that Marmite isn’t really a sauce before dismissing it as “filth”, and moving on to mint sauce."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "a9d08880-fcfa-3218-b721-cf67b0bbc351",
                "word": "marmite",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "e211ce27-330c-443d-a5c8-dd9fe9d02ba0",
                        "entry_id": "a9d08880-fcfa-3218-b721-cf67b0bbc351",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "marmites"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "6a14df1d-9c1e-39d3-9fe3-6f363d18de40",
                        "entry_id": "a9d08880-fcfa-3218-b721-cf67b0bbc351",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A rounded cooking pot of various designs, commonly pot-bellied, with or without tripod, handles, lid etc; originally earthenware but currently more commonly of cast iron or other metals."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "027bff24-fa78-4a3f-9019-462ee1edd1c8",
                                "sense_id": "6a14df1d-9c1e-39d3-9fe3-6f363d18de40",
                                "text": "1824 Thomas Gill. The Technical Repository p. 180: XXXV: On the French Marmite, or Pot-au-Feu: and on preparing Bouillon with it\nMy little boy having been ill of a fever for forty days, I have learned from his attendant how to make the celebrated soup (bouillon) of Paris: and finding it to be superior to any that I ever before tasted, I take the liberty to send you the directions necessary to enable any one to prepare this cheap and desirable food.\nEarthen-pots with covers, made to hold from one to seven pounds of meat, are found in every family. The marmite bought for me was for one-and-a-half pound only: this quantity of lean meat (bœufmaigre), was always part of the leg or shoulder: it was put into the marmite, which was then filled up with cold water, about five pints, and placed on the hearth, close to the wood-fire; and when it began to simmer or boil gently, it threw up a scum, which was carefully taken off from time to time with a spoon, for the space of threequarters of an hour, which perfectly cleansed the meat and water from every impurity."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
                "translations": [],
                "word_linkages": []
            },
            {
                "entry_id": "2be97fdf-2edf-3906-9ff3-61d5f0ace73f",
                "word": "Marmite",
                "pos": "noun",
                "lang_code": "en",
                "forms": [
                    {
                        "form_id": "094c6074-3e4f-46a4-b596-91bd452760e5",
                        "entry_id": "2be97fdf-2edf-3906-9ff3-61d5f0ace73f",
                        "tags": [
                            "plural"
                        ],
                        "form": "Marmites"
                    }
                ],
                "senses": [
                    {
                        "sense_id": "25a994d7-b6f3-3c35-9321-4d11cfbff97a",
                        "entry_id": "2be97fdf-2edf-3906-9ff3-61d5f0ace73f",
                        "glosses": [
                            "Something which people either love or hate."
                        ],
                        "tags": [
                            "UK",
                            "countable",
                            "informal",
                            "uncountable"
                        ],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "25b51ccc-e2fd-4999-8996-fa9783a0a1e4",
                                "sense_id": "25a994d7-b6f3-3c35-9321-4d11cfbff97a",
                                "text": "A pink gin is prepared by swirling a few drops of bitters round the glass and throwing any excess away. It's the Marmite of the drinking world."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "ff494e3e-1091-4000-b789-7b0e4cbc75dd",
                                "sense_id": "25a994d7-b6f3-3c35-9321-4d11cfbff97a",
                                "text": "Ah, anchovies - the marmite of the marine world. Personally, I love them, at least on pizzas."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e4f11a3e-7567-4ba8-ad67-b13dfcfde670",
                                "sense_id": "25a994d7-b6f3-3c35-9321-4d11cfbff97a",
                                "text": "I have a feeling that this pie is the Marmite of the school pie world. You either loved it or you hated it."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "8850e48a-66db-4528-97f5-13e2d5d3c393",
                                "sense_id": "25a994d7-b6f3-3c35-9321-4d11cfbff97a",
                                "text": "Catamarans are the Marmite of powerboating – you either love them or you hate them."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "e8bbe352-13b3-4d3a-b16d-e032b663bba9",
                                "sense_id": "25a994d7-b6f3-3c35-9321-4d11cfbff97a",
                                "text": "A LOT of people could ask me that question. It’s a Marmite thing, you either love him or hate him,” admits Consett actor Chris Coxon who is one of the first people on the planet to attempt to become a real-life version of cult animated character Spongebob Squarepants^([sic])"
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "9b42f916-3f53-41b2-a94e-a84312e97ea9",
                                "sense_id": "25a994d7-b6f3-3c35-9321-4d11cfbff97a",
                                "text": "“I’ve always been quite a Marmite personality,” she says, comparing herself to the divisive umami toast spread."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
